            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Total and irreversible destruction, judgment, or removal, with the specific instances of mountains or trees cast into the sea frequently read as hyperbolic metaphors for faith overcoming impossible obstacles.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The irreversible destruction of a ruling power, which sinks into the sea of peoples and never rises again.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Irreversible destruction, judgment, or complete removal of an entity.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A ruling power irreversibly destroyed and sunk into the sea of peoples.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book narrows the general concept of irreversible destruction specifically to 'ruling powers' and defines the sea as 'peoples.' By doing so, it reclassifies the mountain and tree faith sayings (Matt 21, Luke 17) and the shipwreck narrative (Acts 27) as symbolic judgments of ruling powers, rejecting the dominant consensus readings of impossible obstacles or literal history for those verses.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None at the core level. The book adopts and formalizes a known minority/preterist interpretation within the consensus, elevating it to the exclusive definition across all cited passages.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book refines the consensus by restricting the destroyed object to a 'ruling power' and the destination to 'peoples.' This requires reclassifying texts typically read as individual faith metaphors into apocalyptic or political judgments, which is recognized as an alternative view in the consensus.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The faith sayings in Matthew 21:21 and Luke 17:6 do not explicitly reference ruling powers or judgment; they are framed around faith and prayer. Similarly, Acts 27:41 describes a historical shipwreck without